# Next serial idea: downstream model application scope

Question: after three scoped mechanism branches closed without a new general
algorithm, can low-rank, coreset, or streaming-statistics components deliver a
measurable resource/quality tradeoff for a model task within CPU and8GB, without
weak baselines or novelty inflation?

Begin with a discriminative task rather than a neural generative model: a labeled
sparse/high-dimensional benchmark, convex predictive loss, calibration/error, wall
and CPU time, and peak memory can all be checked on CPU. The initial work is a
source/protocol audit only: choose one public dataset whose native train/test split,
license, labels, and feature construction are reproducible; identify full-data,
uniform/reservoir, sparse exact, and standard dimensionality-reduction baselines;
state whether the candidate component changes training examples, features, or only
evaluation. Do not download or train until this contract is fixed.

Reject a benchmark if its native dimension is so small that exact training is the
obvious dominant baseline, if labels must be synthesized from the same statistic
being evaluated, or if preprocessing leakage cannot be excluded. Any later model
experiment must use a held-out test split, count preprocessing/sketch construction,
and preserve all negative outcomes. A generative application may follow only after
the discriminative scope decision or if the source audit shows no legitimate
discriminative benchmark under the resource constraints.
